{"type": "thinking", "text": "The location is /storage/emulated/0/Documents/markor/Zeta. So the actual root notebook folder is /storage/emulated/0/Documents/markor, and Zeta is under it. But at the file browser root, the \"..\" row described /storage/emulated/0/Documents, not .../markor. Hmm wait, at root the toolbar title was \"Markor\" and row desc /storage/emulated/0/Documents. So the root listing likely was of /storage/emulated/0/Documents/markor, but the description displayed truncated path? That would be a bug: root's \"..\" row shows the wrong path (missing \"/markor\"). Hmm, but maybe the root row is the parent (Documents) and the app is showing the parent row as an \"up\" entry pointing to Documents, since it lets you navigate above the notebook root? At root, \"..\" row had folder icon and description /storage/emulated/0/Documents, i.e., the parent. Then inside Zeta, the \"..\" row should be the parent /storage/emulated/0/Documents/markor. That's correct! So the \"..\" row is always the parent folder. At root, it's Documents (parent of markor). This means the \"..\" description is correct and consistent: root's parent is Documents; Zeta's parent is markor. There's no bug. The app allows navigating above the notebook root.\n\nOK so that's consistent. Good.\n\nNow inside Zeta, inside.md exists (precondition didn't mention it, but fine).\n\nClose details (OK), deselect, go back to root. Then test Search. Then editor settings deeper.\n\nActually, let's prioritize the bugs found. Let me think about which are real and report them:\n\n1. Unordered list dialog title \"Theme\" — clear wrong label. Expected \"Unordered list character\" or similar. This is likely a bug in editor settings. The change note says glyph preference loaded through separate accessor; maybe the dialog title uses a wrong resource. Very likely intentional bug.\n\n2. Unordered list action inserts '-' regardless of configured glyph (tested '*' and '+'). This is likely the intended bug for \"Unordered list action now detects existing list markers in a new way\"? Hmm, that change is about detection, not glyph. But the glyph preference change says \"loaded through a separate accessor\" — maybe the bug is that the action uses the wrong accessor default? Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the unordered list action was never supposed to honor the glyph setting? Let me check actual Markor behavior: In Markor, the unordered list action inserts the character from the preference `pref_key__editor_unordered_list_char`? The setting exists \"Unordered list character - Which character to use to denote unordered lists\". So yes, the action should use it. Bug.\n\n3. New-file extension for todo.txt = \".todo.txt\" — likely bug. But need confidence. Let's reason about Markor source: `DocumentFormat` enum in recent Markor:\n\n```\npublic enum DocumentFormat {\n    MARKDOWN(\"Markdown\", \"md\", ...),\n    TEXT(\"Plain Text\", \"txt\", ...),\n    TODOTXT(\"todo.txt\", \"txt\", ...),\n    WIKITEXT(\"Wikitext / Zim\", \"txt\", ...),\n    ASCIIDOC(\"AsciiDoc\", \"adoc\", ...),\n    ORGMODE(\"OrgMode\", \"org\", ...),\n    CSV(\"CSV\", \"csv\", ...);\n}\n```\n\nSo extension is \"txt\" for TODO. If the new-file dialog builds the extension field from `format.getName()` (display name) instead of `format.getExtension()`, then TODO → \"todo.txt\". Observed \".todo.txt\" exactly matches appending \".\" + name. But what about Markdown → would be \".Markdown\" not \".md\". We observed Markdown? We didn't actually test selecting Markdown after others! Initially extension was \".md\" with Markdown selected (default). Let's test selecting Markdown now to see: if it becomes \".Markdown\", that's a big bug (affects all). If it stays \".md\", then TODO's display name is used... hmm.\n\nActually wait: when Type=Markdown initially, ext=\".md\" (default set by app when opening dialog, maybe from format). We haven't selected Markdown from the spinner after another type. Let's test that: selecting Markdown should set \".md\". If it sets \".Markdown\", then the bug is broad. We must test.\n\nSimilarly Plain Text → \".txt\" (not \".Plain Text\"), Wikitext → \".txt\" (not \".Wikitext / Zim\"), AsciiDoc → \".adoc\" (not \".AsciiDoc\"), OrgMode → \".org\" (not \".OrgMode\"), CSV → \".csv\" (not \".CSV\"). So the mapping is via extension except todo.txt → \".todo.txt\". Why would todo.txt be special? Because its extension field... hmm, if mapping used `format.getExtension()` for all, TODO would be \"txt\". Unless TODO's getExtension() literally returns \"todo.txt\"? Hmm.\n\nWait maybe Wikimedia: \"Wikitext / Zim\" extension is \"txt\"? Actually Markor's Wikitext format extension may be \"wiki\". Observed \".txt\". Hmm, so mapping isn't getExtension then? Let me check known Markor: I recall in Markor's `DocumentFormat`:\n\n```\nMARKDOWN(new MarkdownTextConverter()),\nTEXT(new PlainTextConverter()),\nTODOTXT(new TodoTxtTextConverter()),\nWIKITEXT(new WikitextTextConverter()),\nASCIIDOC(new AsciidocTextConverter()),\nORGMODE(new OrgmodeTextConverter()),\nCSV(new CsvTextConverter());\n```\nand `getExtension()` returns converter.getFormatExtension() maybe. For Wikitext it might be \"txt\". For TodoTxt \"txt\". Good.\n\nSo \".todo.txt\" is likely from a separate string conversion: maybe `format.getFormatName().toLowerCase()`? For Markdown name \"Markdown\" would be \".markdown\" — not observed. Hmm, but we haven't tested Markdown. Let me test now. We need to close info dialog first, go to Files, open new file dialog, select Markdown, check ext.\n\nLet me close ok, deselect, go root, FAB."}
