{"type": "thinking", "text": "This time Folder local + Name checked (pending Name preserved!). Earlier when Folder local was checked and Date checked, tapping Folder local gave Size checked. That contradicts a consistent \"reset to stored\" theory. Let's re-examine: In that earlier sequence, state was Folder local checked + Date checked. Tapping Folder local → Folder local unchecked + Size checked. Now state Folder local unchecked + Name checked; tapping Folder local → Folder local checked + Name checked. So the difference is direction (checking vs unchecking). When *unchecking* Folder local (tap it when checked), the radio changes to Size. When *checking* it, radio stays. Bizarre: unchecking Folder local sets radio to Size.\n\nHypothesis: The underlying sort method stored is \"Folder local\"?? And radio \"Size\" corresponds to sort method index? Hmm.\n\nMaybe the dialog is a multi-choice list where \"Folder local\" is one item in the same group and the app's logic ensures exactly one of the five sort methods is selected. The checked item index is computed by `sortMethod.ordinal()`. The items array order is [Folder local (ordinal 0?), Name(1), Date(2), Size(3), MIME(4), ...]. When you tap an item, Android's single-choice sets its checked state and clears others. But the app's listener also does `sortMethod = values[which]` and persists/updates. When unchecking Folder local, the listener maps index 0 to some method (maybe Folder local), persists it, but then the dialog re-render shows... Size? Hmm.\n\nActually Android's `setSingleChoiceItems` never unchecks the currently checked item by tapping it. But we observed Folder local got unchecked by tapping it, meaning it's NOT in the single-choice group. So Folder local is a separate multi-choice item. And when you tap it (uncheck), the radio selection mysteriously becomes Size. Why Size? Maybe because the checkbox at index 0's click handler triggers `sortMethod = SortMethod.SIZE` due to a bug in the listener's index mapping (off-by-one or wrong array). Specifically, the listener might be the same for all items and compute `values[position]` using a *different* array that excludes \"Folder local\", causing index shift: tapping position 0 (\"Folder local\") maps to values[0] = NAME? Not Size. Hmm, but it selected Size. If values array = [Name, Date, Size, MIME], position 0 → Name, not Size. \n\nMaybe the underlying sort method currently stored is Size; tapping Folder local (a separate pref) causes a re-render of the dialog from the *stored* sort method (Size), which discards the pending Date/Name choice. But in the second test, pending Name was *preserved* after tapping Folder local (checking), so no reset. Unless the re-render only happens when unchecking. Hmm, unchecking Folder local might trigger a preference listener that re-renders the list (since the \"folder local\" mode turns off and it must show the global sort), reading the stored global sort = Size. Checking it might not re-render. That is plausible! When \"Folder local\" is on, the dialog shows folder-local sort? Hmm.\n\nOK this is getting deep. Is this a real user-facing bug worth reporting? The observable: selecting a criterion (e.g., Date) and toggling \"Folder local\" off causes the highlighted criterion to jump from Date to Size, and if you then press OK, Size is applied instead of your chosen Date. Also Cancel doesn't revert the Folder local toggle. These are clear inconsistencies in the sort menu's state handling. Given the change is \"sort choice stored in app settings\", this could be the planted bug.\n\nBut I need to be sure about a simpler, more defensible bug: Cancel not reverting \"Folder local\". Let's reproduce that cleanly and report:\n1. Open Sort by. Note Folder local state (e.g., unchecked).\n2. Tap Folder local (check it). \n3. Tap CANCEL.\n4. Reopen Sort by → Folder local is still checked (change not reverted) — while the criterion choice (e.g., Date) is reverted.\n\nThat's a clear violation of Cancel semantics. But if \"Folder local\" is an immediately-applied toggle by design, Cancel wouldn't apply. However, the dialog's CANCEL typically should revert anything in the dialog. Also, the existence of a separate saved sort choice means the checkbox should be part of the dialog's pending state.\n\nLet's reproduce carefully with current state: Folder local checked + Name checked (pending, not saved). First tap CANCEL → discard pending Name; Folder local should remain checked (saved). Then reopen: expect Folder local checked + Size (stored criterion). Let's confirm."}
